## Children and internal queues

List child modules, owned `INNER` queues, and the data flow between them. State the exact `SetObjectCapacity()` count used in `Build()`. For every queue, name its single output producer and single input consumer. Note any intentional cycle.

## State and cycle behavior

List any typed non-owning module references, their target owners, and method
contracts. Initialize references with BindModule(target) in the constructor or
Build; use target ? BindModule(target) : nullptr for optional absence. Const
targets are supported. References count neither as objects nor as queue ports.
Null/duplicate bindings are rejected; after Build bindings are frozen. Targets
must be registered in this system, though cross-root, cross-hierarchy, self, and
cyclic references are legal. Keep pointers alive and fixed after Build. DOT
renders one dashed caller-to-target edge labeled method per binding, without
port/method names or runtime invocation counts.

The default module embeds `SimState<std::uint64_t> count_`, named
`@MODULE_NAME@.count`, initialized to zero. Include the public `SimState.h`;
no companion State class generation is needed. `count_` records Work calls since
reset. `NextCount(prior)` is a `PURE static` increment. `UpdateState()` stages the
new count and returns it for the `work_cycles=N` debug log; reads still return
the old count until Xfer. ReportStat reports the committed count as `Work cycles`.
Bookkeeping alone does not call MarkProgress or request termination. The smoke
test steps it explicitly and verifies no-progress deadlock at two cycles.

Explicitly call each embedded state's Build, Xfer, Reset, and ReportStat as in
the code template; skip its empty Work. States are not objects in the owner's
topology count and are not child modules, queues, DOT nodes, or worker tasks.
They have no automatic Parent/GetSim context and are not frozen or checked by
system traversal. Multiple states and fixed state arrays are allowed: name and
advance each element separately. Owner Xfer visits N instances in O(N), each checking its own bool.

List private state fields and their reset values. Describe the blocking checks before input consumption, local helper methods, `Work()` behavior, and `Xfer()` bookkeeping. Define the exact event that calls `MarkProgress()`, the completion condition that calls `RequestTerminate()`, and the chosen deadlock threshold.

List every `PURE` method, its explicit inputs and output, and why it is independent of module state. State the complete transaction readiness condition and the commit order: peek, compute, write outputs, then pop inputs. Explain blocked-cycle behavior.

Identify which queues carry scalar values and which carry `std::shared_ptr<Message>`. For pointer messages, name the allocation source and whether downstream modules only forward or intentionally mutate the object.

