| Event or counter | Unit | Trigger or denominator |
| --- | --- | --- |

Use stable logger unit and stage names. Define whether a reported count means events, cycles, queue entries, or bytes. For percentages, name the denominator.

## Trace contract

List all owned queues and their payload formatters. Define any module duration slices or dependency flows, including their start/end cycle events. State whether every-cycle capture is required or an explicit sampling interval is acceptable.

## Tests

List tests for normal flow, blocked cycles, reset, hierarchy, and log/report output.

## Parallel execution and generated functions

Keep ordinary functions as ordinary calls; do not create queue ports for function
arguments. Generate only actual optional ports and bind non-null endpoints in the
constructor. `PURE static` functions receive explicit const inputs and return
results without queue access. Evaluate current state into pending state in
`Work()` and commit it in `Xfer()`; Reset restores current and clears written
flags, without needing to clear pending storage. A blocked local helper returns to
`Work()`, which must still call `WorkChildren()` exactly once. Keep all five
lifecycle methods and the child/queue helper positions shown in the code template.

Document the selected parallel parent and physical queue owners. Its direct
child subtrees run on fixed workers; queue advancement follows the producer
subtree, or the consumer subtree when only that endpoint is on a worker. The
owner helper skips delegated queues. Xfer commits private state and must not
query queues or another module's state; perform readiness checks in Work.
Describe payload ownership after handoff and obey the restricted parallel queue
API in `docs/API/SimQueue.md`. No runtime port or hierarchy changes are allowed.

Embedded state current values stay read-only during Work. Different workers may
write different SimState instances through ordinary pointers or methods on a
BindModule target; the model must prevent concurrent writes to one instance,
including members of a struct value. Sequential writes to one instance use the
last value, and unwritten fields hold their value.
Each state commits once on its owner's thread after all Work completes. Other
tasks must not access it during Xfer. The existing two phase barriers suffice;
there is no state reference-binding API. Module binding only records topology:
methods execute synchronously on the caller's thread when explicitly called,
never automatically from target Work. Use methods for cross-module state access
only during Work, not Xfer. There is no dispatcher, new task, thread switch,
runtime phase guard, conflict detection, or arbitration. Binding changes neither
worker placement nor queue endpoints/access rules.
